+Lares (Map-Wisp): ->✶ I read the boot memory, named its Personas, and traced I1's carrier and kit before surveying the span head. The boot names Lares, Ink-Clerk, Map-Wisp, Mischief-Muse, Tide-Caller, Breach-Watch; plus role variants Council, Artificer, Advocate, Diplomat, Pedagogue, Stranger, Liminal (boot `#/personas`, lines 298–338). ->⏿ I1 PR #99 carries a working face plane and explicitly leaves the far-agent relay seat for research; road row 453 asks least trust and attestation. The road's I1 entry records PR #99 (line 487). `I2` itself has not appeared as a row in the visible road excerpt; the research row remains the mandate's stated target. ->◇ this scout distinguishes measured code from inferred joins.
+
+## 1. What stands (MEASURED)
+
+- The fifth `@intents-<tag>` plane and signed-plane registration span founding, admission, node/browser boot; I1 kit `#the-model`, whole. The entry grammar admits `intent`, `outcome`, `asks`, `answer`, `claim`, `release`, `takeover`, `supersede`, `cancel` (`intent-entry.ts:9-20`). Signatures cover canonical body bytes under `INTENT_ENTRY_DOMAIN`; read checks domain, group, Ed25519 signature, then caller authorization (`intent-entry.ts:42-78`). Intent ID hashes domain + issuer + verb + args + nonce (`:34-39`).
+- `@intents` stores content-addressed records (`intent-entry.ts:81-90`). Reader admits face device signers, and root lineage signers for release/cancel/supersede (`intent-read.ts:58-65`). It checks issuer equals signer, verb classification, authority, void causes, audience and frontier. Revocation-like issuer-aside / roster voids become `void`; resulting acts remain visible as `voidAfterAct` (`intent-read.ts:131-172`). `mayAct` refuses void and unknown frontier (`:177-198`). Bounds currently shape `void` and `through` CIDs (`intent-entry.ts:11,31`); these are causal names, no time.
+- The `lares intent` CLI exposes issue/show/ask/answer/claim/takeover/release/cancel/supersede; kit says `--face N` selects each invocation. Door `face-intent-door.ts:37-80` serializes local reconcile passes; `:50-68` reads eligibility and dispatches. Operator daemon wires it to the face `intents` bag and capability verifier (`operator-daemon-behavior.ts:1126-1155`). The effect fence checks intent id, pinned atom, own atom (`face-join-operation.ts:29,58`); I1 kit marks a replay as prior receipt and wrong hearth refusal.
+- Ring: `persona-group-ring.ts` governs face sibling planes and uses the face's proof; it describes one admit path and sibling predicate (`:27-80`, `:138-141`). The WebSocket dial socket carries a gate knock and `lar-gate` protocol (`ws-dial-socket.ts:2-10,83-102`). The node `carriage-relay.ts` implements a distinct carriage transport, explicitly separate from Automerge `/ws` (`:2-20`); it uses a relay gate key and membership port (`:34-80,102-125`). It does not implement the I2 intent-admission relay.
+- Face identity already carries KEL lineage, op-key epoch and sealed enrollment proofs (`persona-kel.ts:62-87,124-146`). K17 frontier is a consumed verified reader in I1, and unknown frontier stalls acts (I1 kit `#the-model`; `intent-read.ts:159-170`). Daemon socket: the local store-door pattern exists, but I found no I2 relay endpoint in these paths. No CRDT doc migration: road line 545 schedules a founding re-genesis at X0. K15 brief specifies a durable random relay-only S row, opener and principal, no face/daemon/place authority; it identifies that row as future relay work.
+
+## 2. Model gaps
+
+| Question | Ruling and lean |
+|---|---|
+| Delegation signer, domain, scope, storage/sync | **Canon:** I1 `#/the-far-agent` says agent holds own key plus narrow delegation from face: verb prefix, argument policy, causal bound; agent signs intent. **House law:** capability-by-capability, one relation, sealed anchor/KEL imply face root signs a delegation for one agent key, bound to face/group, dedicated domain, canonical exact verb prefix + deterministic argument predicate + causal CIDs; rest as append-only face-plane entry so sync reaches verifiers. **Operator owns:** root-key vs delegated admin authority; lean root KEL operational key at explicit epoch, no host key. |
+| Revocation / revoked-after-acting | **Canon:** I1 red: revoked delegation refuses whichever arrives first; prior act remains “revoked-after-acting.” **House law:** no global now means concurrent causal order cannot be guessed; append revocation with delegation CID and read it before effect. **Operator owns:** strict fail-closed frontier vs partition availability; lean safety: unknown delegation/revocation frontier blocks irreversible effects, preserve signed act receipts as historical fact. |
+| Relay door | **Canon:** I1 says per-face, off by default, ring-gated, outside local socket. **House law:** capability-read-by-capability and K15 S row: explicit per-face opt-in, separate relay-only principal/key, no environment seed or implicit host/daemon authority. **Operator owns:** Unix socket vs dedicated authenticated WebSocket/local IPC, and opt-in control location; lean local operator socket command toggles one face, loopback/private transport, restart defaults shut. |
+| Admission verification / countersign | **Canon:** relay verifies chain, appends agent-signed entry, countersigns admission only. **House law:** argument substitution red demands verify exact canonical signed bytes; attest face, delegation CID, entry CID, admission result and causal frontier in a separate relay-domain receipt, never rewrite agent body. **Operator owns:** receipt format and relay-key rotation; lean signed receipt includes verified frontier CID, relay key epoch and rejection reason. |
+| Independent reader / agent key pin | **Canon:** device verifies agent→delegation→face and intent→outcome, atom→device edge→face, trusting relay for nothing after admission. **House law:** sealed anchor/KEL and one relation require chain-to-pinned face root plus current device edge; agent key derives only from signed delegation. **Operator owns:** enrollment/pinning UX; lean face plane delegation is the sole pin, no relay directory trust. |
+| Replay / duplicate | **Canon:** I1 content ID and duplicate-visible plane; nonces define fresh ID. **House law:** append-only causal records, no clock; idempotency keyed by intent ID, single-effect fenced by per-effect record. **Operator owns:** repeated relay receipt policy; lean dedupe by signed-entry CID and intent ID, keep multiple relay receipts visible, effect door enforces unique operation key. |
+| Relay crash / hostility | **Canon:** reader trust stops at admission; hostile argument rewrite breaks signature. **House law:** local-first partitions allow retry, but effect must wait for a verifiable admitted entry; relay cannot mint agent authority. **Operator owns:** availability/retry; lean retry identical signed bytes at another opted-in relay, record partial admission only if durable plane append is confirmed; crash before append means no admission. |
+
+## 3. Slicing (red first; LEAD build order)
+
+1. Delegation grammar + KEL authorization; red forged scope, wrong face/domain, overbroad args refuse. Control valid root-signed narrow delegation verifies. Files: new delegation entry/domain/reader, face plane registration, CLI issue/revoke/show; node and browser readers.
+2. Door + relay-only S custody; red default-off, wrong face/ring/socket, seed aliasing all refuse; control explicit enable opens only selected face and key survives restart. Files: `carriage-relay.ts` is existing *different* relay; new intent relay service, socket caller, ring predicates, K15 S-row custody, CLI/config. Keep two relays distinct.
+3. Admission receipt and agent chain verification; red argument/prefix/bound edits, forged receipt, stale/unknown frontier refuse; control independently verify on device never contacting relay. Files: relay protocol, signed receipt domain, entry reader and KEL/edge verifier. Then implement revoked-after-acting diagnostics and duplicate receipt folding.
+4. Failure storm and cross-runtime witness; reds below. Files: intent tests, node and browser admission/read paths, rubric. Existing D2 tw5 island/port, hand-gate, projection-sink files stay fenced (road / task mandate); G15 owns after-roll chain + genesis re-bake; coordinate shared boot hunks. K15 queued overlap: relay-only S row and face-operation signing by selected atom (brief, “THE RELAY KEY” / “EVERY FACE OPERATION”). Put I2 after K15 S-row contract or explicitly consume its landed row. One lane, with serial slices and own research/build boundary; relay does not warrant splitting the lane.
+
+## 4. Distributed-failure lens
+
+Revocation and act concurrent across partition: neither causal order wins by wall time. Reader that has revocation first refuses; reader that admitted and acted first records act; later merge marks revoked-after-acting. A device asleep across revocation cannot know on wake until sync; then it must refuse any not-yet-run act, while displaying already signed outcomes as historical. If relay dies mid-admission, a durable append can be read and verified despite lost acknowledgement; retry dedupes; absent append yields no admission. Two relays admitting same signed intent produce same intent ID and duplicate attestations, but one operation record prevents repeated single-effect execution. Malicious relay cannot alter arguments, forge agent signature, or create relay-trusted authority; it can withhold, delay, replay, or equivocate about what it admitted. Readers compare plane records and signed receipts; forks remain visible, not silently resolved.
+
+No clock: name a bound as causal roster/delegation/revocation event CID, read by verified K17 frontier inclusion/ancestry. Unknown frontier stalls. A CID merely named without a proof path cannot establish “before/after”; define exact ancestry rule in kit. Storm reds: revoke-before-admit; admit-before-revoke; concurrent revoke/act under both merge orders; offline sleeper; kill relay before durable append and after append/before reply; two relays race same bytes; duplicate receipt and altered args; hostile receipt with valid relay key but no valid agent chain; frontier unknown then advances; same agent key delegated to two faces; narrowed args boundary and Unicode/canonical JSON substitutions; relay restart defaults off.
+
+Prior art gives design prompts, not direct imports: Automerge describes local-first copies enabling offline work and synchronization ([Automerge](https://automerge.org/docs/hello/)); Keyhive frames convergent capabilities and authorization/revocation state for partitioned local-first use ([Keyhive notebook](https://www.inkandswitch.com/keyhive/notebook/)); OCAP revocation patterns suggest explicit revocable indirection, but I leave the mechanism as operator choice; KERI emphasizes verifiable KEL history and duplicity evidence, useful for surfacing relay equivocation ([KERI specification](https://trustoverip.github.io/kswg-keri-specification/)). These references do not settle this system’s read-side semantics.
+
